If you own a rental, or you're buying a home with a basement suite, you've probably assumed the rent will help you qualify for the mortgage. It will. But lenders almost never count 100% of it, and the way they count it can make a surprisingly big difference.

## Why Lenders Don't Count All Your Rent

Rent isn't guaranteed. Units sit empty, tenants stop paying, and properties need repairs, taxes and insurance. So lenders build in a cushion by only counting part of your gross rental income, typically somewhere between 50% and 80%, depending on the lender and the property.

## Two Different Methods

### Add-back (often around 50%)

The lender adds a portion of the rent to your income. Your full mortgage payment and property costs stay on the expense side of your debt ratios.

### Rental offset (often up to 80%)

The lender uses a portion of the rent to cancel out the property's expenses directly. The rent is subtracted from the costs instead of being added to your income.

**Why it matters:** because debt ratios are percentages, the offset method usually helps you qualify for more than the add-back method, even when the rent is the same. Two lenders looking at the same $2,000/month suite can come to very different borrowing limits.

## What Else Affects How Your Rent Is Counted

* **Proof of rent:** leases, bank deposits and your tax returns showing rental income.
* **The type of property:** a legal secondary suite is treated differently from an unofficial basement apartment.
* **Number of properties:** some lenders tighten their rules as your portfolio grows.
* **Gross vs. net:** lenders usually start from gross rent, not the net figure after expenses you report to the CRA.

## Why This Matters If You're Growing a Portfolio

Each lender has its own policy, which is exactly why a broker matters for investors. The same file can be a "no" at one bank and a comfortable "yes" at another lender that uses a more generous rental method. For investors in Barrie, Innisfil and across Simcoe County, where secondary suites and student or family rentals are common, choosing the right lender for each purchase can decide how many properties you can eventually own.

